#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6defc05f-0323-58b2-906c-c926cb3a64a5" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using the Modified Euler Method"}] :question/id #uuid "8cd11f2b-5a59-5ac9-8f29-e059ed5b1a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-21274" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy - y^2 + 2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= xy - y^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 - 1^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 1 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= f(x_{new}, y_{p}) \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{p} - y_{p}^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= (1)(2) - (2)^{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(1 + 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The Modified Euler Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "237cb58e-d160-5bda-94c4-5727f1be79b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21275" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x^2y^2+xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{2}y^{2} + xy^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot 1^{2} + 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 1 + 2 = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new}^{2}y_{p}^{2} + x_{new}y_{p}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 + 54) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 28 = 29\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n| $2$ | $29$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{29}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166447 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d78a28df-7cbf-5079-8c99-b9dac6ced696" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the Modified Euler Method"}] :question/id #uuid "0e284d46-a013-5300-b0a6-c220bcde24db" :question/math-academy-id "e-21277" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy_{p} = y^{′} \\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy + y^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1 + 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "216b8f73-2b43-544f-a3c9-ad76db33c4f8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"}] :question/id #uuid "4ca52c79-6683-512c-a2e5-fa7e03157752" :question/math-academy-id "e-21560" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = xy^2 - x^2, \\qquad y(0)=2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)\n$$\n\nis the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point\n\n$$\n(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{p}^{′})Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(0 + \\frac{7}{4}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{16}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "216b8f73-2b43-544f-a3c9-ad76db33c4f8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "0476d33c-a1ea-5a78-86f3-61231837277b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164000}] :question/id #uuid "f276ad41-7dfe-5d1d-ac7b-be9c577fbacc" :question/math-academy-id "q-323980" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x^{2} - y^{2}, y(0) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"completing-the-first-row-of-a-table-using-the-modified-euler-method\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6defc05f-0323-58b2-906c-c926cb3a64a5" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using the Modified Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b65bdb4-c7a4-5097-adc9-08fd0af5cd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164002} {:answer/id #uuid "b39dec20-bffd-5357-8529-fbda1ee976d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164003} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee6f031-bbed-5552-888c-f5ed1b744150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164004} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1743fa-a78c-5711-afe4-60c7922b8edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164005} {:answer/id #uuid "4e087b7a-ebc3-5313-8bfd-cb632c5ede4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b39dec20-bffd-5357-8529-fbda1ee976d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164003} :answer-field/id #uuid "24466b64-d56c-58a3-a382-a88cb5fe891f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164001}] :question/id #uuid "4e0eb5a0-8dd3-54db-baaf-7d7b326d8b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-324062" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2xy, y(1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. 